  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "DSH Doctor",
      "text": "Deterministic diagnostics and recovery for [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness)."
    },
    {
      "section": "DSH Doctor",
      "text": "DSH Doctor is the safety net for the two ways a Harness usually breaks itself: it **cannot converse** (the Web UI opens but the loop is broken), or it **cannot start** (boot fails after a config, dependency, or plugin change). When the Web UI still opens, the Doctor button calls a loopback-only Host recovery service. When Harness cannot start, the same engine runs as a standalone CLI — including a boot probe that captures the failure and a one-click reset to the last healthy checkpoint, so plugin developers can experiment and always get back to a working state."
    },
    {
      "section": "DSH Doctor",
      "text": "> DSH Doctor 0.2.0 targets `@deepseek-ai/dsh 0.1.0-rc.6`. DeepSeek Harness is a developer preview; unknown versions are scanned in conservative read-only mode.\n\n[简体中文](./README.zh-CN.md)"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "Add Doctor to the Web profile:\n\n```bash\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-doctor\ndsh --profile web\n```\n\nThis installs one package with both Host and browser halves. It contributes:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "- a persistent Doctor action beside Settings in the sidebar;\n- an automatic recovery banner above the composer after a prompt or Agent failure;\n- a full Doctor page in Settings with findings, checkpoints, and rollback;\n- a floating emergency entry that appears in the frame overlay whenever the profile is broken, independent of the sidebar and conversation plugins.\n\nIf the Web UI cannot start:\n\n```bash\nnpx dsh-doctor recover --profile web\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "CLI",
      "text": "```bash"
    },
    {
      "section": "Scan one profile (offline and path-anonymized by default)",
      "text": "npx dsh-doctor scan"
    },
    {
      "section": "Probe whether the Harness can boot and diagnose the failure from its output",
      "text": "npx dsh-doctor boot"
    },
    {
      "section": "dependencies, then verify the boot again",
      "text": "npx dsh-doctor recover --profile web\nnpx dsh-doctor recover --profile web --yes   # also apply confirmed actions"
    },
    {
      "section": "Snapshot the current profile as a healthy recovery baseline",
      "text": "npx dsh-doctor checkpoint --profile web"
    },
    {
      "section": "Start the Harness; diagnose automatically when it fails to boot",
      "text": "npx dsh-doctor launch --auto-recover"
    },
    {
      "section": "Restore a checkpoint",
      "text": "npx dsh-doctor rollback <checkpoint-id>\n```\n\n`recover` runs the safe actions with zero confirmation, then verifies the result by booting the Harness when it was down. The exit status is `1` when errors remain (or boot verification fails), warnings return `0` unless `--strict` is used, and invalid arguments or internal failures return `2`. JSON output is available with `--json`."
    },
    {
      "section": "What v0.2.0 checks",
      "text": "- Node, platform, Harness version and profile discovery;\n- profile manifest, bundle shape, `cordis.yml`, `cordis.patch.yml`, and pnpm workspace syntax and permissions;\n- missing dependencies, duplicate bundles, and broken profile links;\n- live Cordis plugin phases when called from the Web UI;\n- boot failures via a real boot probe (port watch + captured output mapped to repair actions);\n- session records (`session.jsonl.zstd`) and storage caches for corruption;\n- recent local fatal/plugin-failure log markers;\n- non-registry and external plugin sources (flagged, but never blocking recovery);\n- model route/credential state and an optional, tiny, non-session probe that verifies the model actually answers."
    },
    {
      "section": "Recovery",
      "text": "Every mutation follows the same transaction:\n\n1. Re-check the plan expiry and hashes of profile files.\n2. Create a private pre-repair checkpoint.\n3. Apply only selected actions.\n4. Run a fresh structural scan (and boot verification when the Harness was down).\n5. Roll back automatically when verification fails."
    },
    {
      "section": "Recovery",
      "text": "Safe one-click actions prepare Doctor state and **reset the profile configuration to the latest healthy checkpoint**. On a cold start with no healthy checkpoint, Doctor can synthesize minimal valid config files — after explicit confirmation, with the broken originals preserved in the pre-repair checkpoint. Dependency installation, disabling a boot-crashing plugin, quarantining a corrupt session file, and re-enabling a Doctor-disabled plugin require explicit confirmation. Doctor refuses to disable the connection, layout, settings, conversation, runtime, module-loader, or Doctor rescue entries."
    },
    {
      "section": "Recovery",
      "text": "Checkpoints are stored locally under `$DSH_HOME/doctor/checkpoints/`, with ten retained per profile and the newest healthy checkpoint always kept. They may contain exact copies of local configuration so that rollback is byte-accurate; they are never included in reports or uploaded. API keys are never printed, changed, or sent by Doctor.\n\nSee [Recovery matrix](./docs/RECOVERY_MATRIX.md) and [Security policy](./SECURITY.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "Requires Node 24 and pnpm.\n\n```bash\npnpm install\npnpm check\nnpm pack --dry-run\n```"
